DAY 20 - PART A: THEORY
CNI Plugins: How Pod Networking Is Implemented
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. WHAT IS CNI?
---------------
CNI (Container Network Interface) is a small standard:
  - the kubelet/container runtime calls a CNI binary with ADD when a
    pod starts and DEL when it stops
  - the binary sets up the pod's interface, IP, and routes, and
    returns the result as JSON

On each node:
  /etc/cni/net.d/      config (which plugin, which IP range)
  /opt/cni/bin/        plugin binaries (bridge, host-local, loopback,
                       portmap, plus your CNI's own)

Most CNIs also run a DaemonSet (an agent on every node) to program
routes, tunnels, or eBPF, and to enforce NetworkPolicies.


2. IPAM
-------
IP Address Management: which IP does the pod get?
  host-local    each node hands out IPs from its PodCIDR
  calico-ipam   blocks of IPs assigned dynamically
  cloud IPAM    IPs from the VPC subnet (AWS VPC CNI)


