4. OVERLAY vs ROUTED vs NATIVE
------------------------------
Overlay (VXLAN/Geneve)  pod packet wrapped inside a node-to-node UDP
                        packet. Works on any network. Small overhead,
                        smaller MTU.
Routed (BGP)            nodes tell each other (or the routers) which
                        pod ranges they own. No wrapping, faster;
                        network must allow it.
Native cloud            pods get real VPC IPs; cloud routes them.
                        Uses more VPC IPs.


5. eBPF
-------
eBPF lets programs run safely inside the Linux kernel. Cilium (and
Calico eBPF mode) use it to route packets, load-balance Services, and
enforce policies without long iptables chains. Faster at scale and
gives deep visibility (which pod talked to which, HTTP paths, drops).


6. CHOOSING
-----------
Learning:      kindnet/Flannel (simple)
Policies:      Calico or Cilium
Cloud:         usually the provider default (Week 7)
Big scale +
observability: Cilium


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- CNI = standard; plugin binaries in /opt/cni/bin, config in
  /etc/cni/net.d.
- Overlay vs routed vs native cloud networking.
- NetworkPolicy enforcement depends on the CNI.
